From left to right the input pairs are `00`, `10`, `11`, `01`, `00` and `01`. The output is high only when the inputs differ.

Place the following Arduino Mega 2560 program segments inside `setup()`, `loop()`, or separate functions, with `Serial.begin(9600)` before printing. Use `PINx` to sample a pin. Use `PORTx` to read or modify the output latch.

```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// (i) Sample PB3. Supply a defined external logic level.
DDRB &= ~_BV(DDB3);
PORTB &= ~_BV(PORTB3);
const bool pb3 = (PINB & _BV(PINB3)) != 0;

// (ii) Set PA2, clear PA5 and preserve the other latch bits.
PORTA = (PORTA | _BV(PORTA2)) & ~_BV(PORTA5);
DDRA |= _BV(DDA2) | _BV(DDA5);

// (iii) Shift the PORTC latch left by a program-selected amount.
const uint8_t shift = 3;
const uint8_t previous = PORTC;
PORTC = (shift < 8) ? static_cast<uint8_t>(previous << shift) : 0;

// (iv) Place ASCII '6' (0x36) on the eight PORTA outputs.
PORTA = static_cast<uint8_t>('6');
DDRA = 0xFF;

// (v) Place the commands to be measured between timestamps.
DDRB |= _BV(DDB7);    // Configure the example output before timing
const uint32_t startUs = micros();
PORTB ^= _BV(PORTB7);  // Example observable operation
const uint32_t elapsedUs = micros() - startUs;
Serial.println(elapsedUs);  // Serial.begin(9600) runs in setup()
```

The exercise does not specify the direction in (iii). Right shift uses `>>`. For (v), subtraction is safe across the unsigned timestamp rollover for intervals shorter than one rollover. At 16 MHz the Arduino Mega's `micros()` advances in 4 µs steps, so repeat a very short operation many times or count CPU cycles to resolve sub-tick times. Keep serial printing outside the measurement.

Assume an ATmega2560 board running the 16 MHz Arduino core. Provide defined logic levels at `PD0` and `PD1`, for example external pull-downs and switches to +5 V. Share ground. Drive `PD7` as specified. On a standard Mega header, `PD7` is not generally broken out, so this pin requires access to the MCU pin or a suitable breakout. Use the MCU port names, which differ from Arduino's D-pin numbering.

With `PD1` as the most significant bit, each switch word sets the period $T=1/f$ and high time $T_H=DT$. Read both inputs once per cycle.

| PD1 PD0 | Period (µs) | High (µs) | Low (µs) |
| :---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 00 | 250000 | 200000 | 50000 |
| 01 | 20000 | 16000 | 4000 |
| 10 | 250000 | 125000 | 125000 |
| 11 | 20000 | 10000 | 10000 |

```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

constexpr uint8_t selectorMask = _BV(PD1) | _BV(PD0);
constexpr uint8_t pulseMask = _BV(PD7);
constexpr uint32_t periodUs[] = {250000, 20000, 250000, 20000};
constexpr uint32_t highUs[] = {200000, 16000, 125000, 10000};

void setup() {
    DDRD &= ~selectorMask;
    PORTD &= ~(selectorMask | pulseMask);
    DDRD |= pulseMask;
}

static void waitUntilElapsed(uint32_t startUs, uint32_t intervalUs) {
    while (static_cast<uint32_t>(micros() - startUs) < intervalUs) {
        // Keep the same timing origin for both edges.
    }
}

void loop() {
    const uint8_t setting = PIND & selectorMask;
    const uint32_t startUs = micros();
    PORTD |= pulseMask;
    waitUntilElapsed(startUs, highUs[setting]);
    PORTD &= ~pulseMask;
    waitUntilElapsed(startUs, periodUs[setting]);
}
```

This is a software pulse generator derived from the GPIO notes. Timing has normal polling and clock-resolution error. Switch changes take effect on the next cycle.

Assume $f_{\mathrm{CPU}}=16\,\mathrm{MHz}$ and take the pulse from PA4 (Arduino Mega D26), the GPIO used by the notes' CTC example. A 2 Hz, 50% waveform has a period of 0.5 s. Toggle the output every 0.25 s. Use Timer5 in CTC mode, prescaler $N=256$.

$$f_{\rm tick}=16{,}000{,}000/256=62{,}500~\mathrm{Hz},\qquad
\mathrm{OCR5A}=0.25(62{,}500)-1=15{,}624.$$

Timer5 counts from 0 through `OCR5A`, sets the compare flag and restarts the count. Poll and clear the flag on each match. Disable any library that already uses Timer5.

```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

constexpr uint8_t pulseMask = _BV(PA4);

void setup() {
    PORTA &= ~pulseMask;
    DDRA |= pulseMask;

    TCCR5B = 0;                       // Stop Timer5 during setup
    TCCR5A = 0;                       // GPIO supplies the output
    TIMSK5 = 0;                       // Poll the match flag
    TCNT5 = 0;
    OCR5A = 15624;
    TIFR5 = _BV(OCF5A);               // Write one to clear stale flag
    TCCR5B = _BV(WGM52) | _BV(CS52);  // CTC mode 4, clk / 256
}

void loop() {
    if (TIFR5 & _BV(OCF5A)) {
        TIFR5 = _BV(OCF5A);
        PORTA ^= pulseMask;
    }
}
```

Use ADC0 on PF0, Arduino Mega A0, as a single-ended input with common ground. Choose AVCC (nominally 5 V) as the reference and leave the 10-bit result right-adjusted. A 16 MHz oscillator divided by 128 gives $f_{\rm ADC}=125\,\mathrm{kHz}$. A normal conversion takes 13 ADC clocks:

$$t_{\rm conv}=13/(125{,}000)=104\,\mu\mathrm{s}.$$

The first conversion after enabling the ADC takes 25 clocks, about 200 µs. Alignment to the ADC clock, register access and polling add a small amount. The lecture sketch reports about 110 µs for a measured normal conversion. Serial output is excluded from the timed interval and slows the effective loop sampling rate.

```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

constexpr uint8_t adcMode =
    _BV(ADEN) | _BV(ADPS2) | _BV(ADPS1) | _BV(ADPS0);

void setup() {
    DDRF &= ~_BV(PF0);
    PORTF &= ~_BV(PF0);                // No internal pull-up
    ADCSRB = 0;                       // MUX5 = 0
    ADMUX = _BV(REFS0);                // AVCC, ADC0, right-adjusted
    ADCSRA = adcMode | _BV(ADIF);      // Enable ADC and clear stale flag
    Serial.begin(9600);
}

void loop() {
    const uint32_t startUs = micros();
    ADCSRA = adcMode | _BV(ADSC);
    while ((ADCSRA & _BV(ADIF)) == 0) {
        // Wait for a new result.
    }
    const uint8_t low = ADCL;
    const uint8_t high = ADCH;         // Read after ADCL
    const uint16_t sample = (static_cast<uint16_t>(high) << 8) | low;
    const uint32_t elapsedUs = micros() - startUs;

    ADCSRA = adcMode | _BV(ADIF);      // Clear completion flag
    Serial.print("ADC = ");
    Serial.print(sample);
    Serial.print(", time = ");
    Serial.print(elapsedUs);
    Serial.println(" us");
}
```

Read bit 5 of **PORTA** at `PA5` (Arduino Mega D27), not Arduino pin D5. Use `PB7` (D13) for an active-high LED and the eight `PORTC` pins for parallel output.

**Synchronization assumption.** The question provides one data line and no clock. Follow the notes' paced-sampling procedure: the source supplies the next bit in each 500 ms LED-off window and holds it through the sampling instant. It can be a manual source responding to the LED or another circuit timed to this pattern. At each sample, shift the accumulated byte right and put the new input in bit 7. After eight samples the first received bit is at bit 0.

```text
PA5 → sampled bit → right-shifting register → PORTC
                               │
                               └── bit/byte indication → PB7 LED
```

```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

constexpr uint8_t dataMask = _BV(PA5);
constexpr uint8_t ledMask = _BV(PB7);

void setup() {
    DDRA &= ~dataMask;
    PORTA &= ~dataMask;   // Well-defined external logic source
    PORTB &= ~ledMask;
    DDRB |= ledMask;
    PORTC = 0;
    DDRC = 0xFF;
}

void loop() {
    uint8_t received = 0;
    for (uint8_t bit = 0; bit < 8; ++bit) {
        PORTB &= ~ledMask;
        delay(500);      // Allow the external source to set its next bit
        received >>= 1;
        if (PINA & dataMask) {
            received |= 0x80;
        }

        PORTB |= ledMask;
        if (bit == 7) {
            PORTC = received;
            delay(5000);  // Extend final bit's indication to 5 s total
        } else {
            delay(2000);
        }
    }
    PORTB &= ~ledMask;
}
```

The final high interval is extended from the normal 2 s to 5 s, as the note's final-byte indication extends its final bit. If the examiner intends a separate 5 s indication, insert a short off gap after the final 2 s pulse and then issue a new 5 s pulse.

Connect active-high LEDs to PB7 (Mega D13) and PA7 (Mega D29), with series resistors where the LEDs are external. Assume a 16 MHz Mega 2560. In the foreground, toggle PB7 every 5 ms. This gives 5 ms high and 5 ms low, or 100 Hz. Timer3 is independent of the foreground. Use CTC rather than reloading an overflow counter in the ISR. With a prescaler of 256, the timer gets 62,500 ticks per second:

$$\mathrm{OCR3A}=62{,}500-1=62{,}499.$$

```text
Main loop  ──────────────→ PB7   toggle every 5 ms
Timer3 CTC → COMPA ISR ──→ PA7   toggle every 1 s
```

```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <avr/interrupt.h>

constexpr uint8_t foregroundLED = _BV(PB7);
constexpr uint8_t interruptLED = _BV(PA7);
constexpr uint32_t halfPeriodUs = 5000;
static uint32_t lastEdgeUs;

ISR(TIMER3_COMPA_vect) {
    PORTA ^= interruptLED;
}

void setup() {
    PORTB &= ~foregroundLED;
    DDRB |= foregroundLED;
    PORTA &= ~interruptLED;
    DDRA |= interruptLED;

    cli();
    TCCR3B = 0;
    TCCR3A = 0;
    TCNT3 = 0;
    OCR3A = 62499;
    TIFR3 = _BV(OCF3A);              // Clear a pending compare flag
    TIMSK3 = _BV(OCIE3A);
    TCCR3B = _BV(WGM32) | _BV(CS32); // CTC mode 4, clk / 256
    sei();

    lastEdgeUs = micros();
}

void loop() {
    if (static_cast<uint32_t>(micros() - lastEdgeUs) >= halfPeriodUs) {
        lastEdgeUs += halfPeriodUs;
        PORTB ^= foregroundLED;
    }
}
```

Compare A interrupts occur at 1 s intervals. The PA7 LED therefore completes one full blink cycle in 2 s.
